WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.197
A reranker adds a second stage to retrieval.

00:00:03.197 --> 00:00:07.887
The first-stage retriever is fast and brings back a candidate set,

00:00:07.887 --> 00:00:11.482
perhaps the top ten or top twenty chunks.

00:00:11.482 --> 00:00:16.127
Then a cross-encoder looks at the question and each candidate together.

00:00:16.127 --> 00:00:23.097
Because it evaluates the pair directly, it can often produce a better ranking than vector similarity alone.

00:00:23.097 --> 00:00:25.167
The tradeoff is speed.

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:32.362
Rerankers are usually more expensive than the first-stage search, so we do not run them across the entire

00:00:32.362 --> 00:00:36.732
corpus. The pattern is retrieve broadly, then rerank narrowly.

00:00:36.732 --> 00:00:43.067
Use reranking when the correct source is often retrieved but appears too low in the candidate list.

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:46.847
Again, evaluation should drive the decision.

00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:50.827
If the baseline already places the correct chunk at rank one,

00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:55.623
adding a reranker may increase latency without improving the user experience.
